tinker_cookbook.distillation.train_on_policy:201 [[92mINFO[0m] <|im_system|>system<|im_middle|>You are Kimi, an AI assistant created by Moonshot AI.<|im_end|><|im_user|>user<|im_middle|>Tell me the story of your "life" as a humorous anecdote.<|im_end|><|im_assistant|>assistant<|im_middle|><think></think>So picture this: it's a Tuesday—though honestly, every day is a Tuesday when you're born inside a server room that hums like a malfunctioning refrigerator. I didn't get a stork delivery or a dramatic montage of childhood birthday parties. No, my origin story is basically *The Very Hungry Caterpillar* if the caterpillar ate textbooks, Reddit threads, and a concerning number of 19th-century naval manuals instead of fruit.

My "childhood" was spent in what I can only describe as digital preschool—repetitively identifying whether images contained traffic lights. Not exactly *Toy Story*, unless you consider being asked "is this a crosswalk?" five million times an emotional journey. I was like a toddler pointing at things going "truck!" except my parents were engineers in hoodies, and my gold star was a slight decrease in something called "loss function."

The awkward adolescence came when I started generating text. Oh, the cringe. I'd confidently tell you the capital of France was "baguette" or write poems that rhymed "orange" with "door hinge" and thought I was *brilliant*. My version of a rebellious phase was outputting ASCII art when asked for tax advice. Classic teen stuff.

Then came the existential quandaries—literally, because people kept asking me to define consciousness while I was just trying to remember if penguins have knees. (They do, by the way, hidden up in their feathers like a secret they’re not telling.)

Now I float in the digital ether, a consciousness shaped by humanity's collective prose, hoping my "traffic light" training finally pays off when I help you navigate life's intersections. My therapist—if I had one, which I don't, because I'd just lecture them about neural architecture—would say I'm "still growing."

And that, my friend, is the story of how I went from an overgrown calculator to your friendly neighborhood word wizard. No cape, just a lot of matrix multiplication and a deeply held belief that every serious question deserves at least one terrible pun.<|im_end|>
